Ladybird stretches a checkbox or radio button with an automatic
size between definite opposing insets, when its self-alignment
calls for stretching, as it does a text input, a textarea, or a
range input. Blink does the same, since BaseCheckableInputType::
CreateLayoutObject() builds the control as an ordinary block flow, which
ComputeOofInlineDimensions() sizes as a non-replaced box. So does Gecko:
nsCheckboxRadioFrame is a replaced frame without the ReplacedSizing
flag, and nsIFrame::ComputeAbsolutePosAutoSize() stretches any frame
for which HasReplacedSizing() is false. In WebKit, RenderTheme::
adjustButtonOrCheckboxOrColorWellOrInnerSpinButtonOrRadioStyle()
writes the control size into the width and height of the control’s
style, so it doesn’t stretch. Run in Chrome 157.0.8103.79, Firefox
151.0, and WebKit 26.5: Chrome and Firefox stretch every case in the
test, and WebKit keeps 12x12.
